},
"title": "Does the specifications pass write the element page, or is it derived when read?",
"question": "Should the specifications pass itself write an element-level determination (a ramp as a whole), or should the page be derived at read time from the parameters' determinations?",
"why_yours": "You said the derivation is what you expect the specifications pass to be doing: adjudicating, deriving and synthesizing across rows of related evidence. That can mean either.",
"context": "The proposal for an element page (T-7) is a view and a renderer option. It reads the live determinations of the parameters a term points to, and holds no value and no item code of its own. Nothing is stored for the element.\n\nYour note says the specifications pass is where adjudicating, deriving and synthesizing across related rows happens. If the pass itself writes an element-level determination, the element becomes a stored, versioned engine output like a parameter's determination. That is a new kind of row and more code, and it must still never be a container that figures are filed into.\n\nA cross-parameter synthesis table was deferred by a ruling (R17) until a second parameter holds an extraction. The decision on this question is where that deferral ends.",
"blocks": "The element pointer (T-7) and its data (D-6).",
"options": [
{
"key": "derived-at-read",
"label": "Derived when read, as proposed",
"effect": "T-7 is a view and a renderer option over the parameters' determinations. Nothing is stored for the element. The synthesis across parameters happens in the view and the page text."
},
{
"key": "written-by-pass",
"label": "The specifications pass writes it",
"effect": "The engine writes an element-level determination from the related parameters' determinations. It is a new stage output with a table and a rule version, built in a tooling pull request, and it ends R17's deferral for this case."
},
{
"key": "other",
"label": "Something else",
"effect": "Say what in the note."
}
],
"source": "Ruling OR-14 of 2026-10-29 (ledger); resolution report section 2 (OR-14)"
}
