},
"title": "Which marker does a floor claim read when a study confirms it?",
"question": "When a study confirms a regulation's value by finding no reason to change it, which marker does the determination read?",
"why_yours": "Your ruling makes the study's absence a confirmation. The marker is what a reader sees, and the present rule holds a regulation-only claim at the empty circle.",
"context": "You ruled that when the tested values can be found, the absence of a prescribed change confirms them, and that a study whose tested values cannot be found has no value.\n\nA regulation-only determination reads ○ (grey, thin or unverified) and migration 116 holds it there. The engine already gives a confirmed code the band of the study that confirms it, so a T1 study that confirms a T6 value lifts it to the study's band. The unsettled part is the case you ruled on: every row of the confirming study is an absence, so no finding gives direction.\n\nNo source states a preference between the options below.",
"blocks": "The REF-00980 cell in the first render (S-1) and the engine change that follows your ruling.",
"options": [
{
"key": "study-band",
"label": "The confirming study's own band",
"effect": "The cell reads what the engine already gives a confirmed code: the band of the verified, retrieved study that confirmed it. Migration 116's rule for regulation-only cells does not apply to it."
},
{
"key": "lower",
"label": "A lower marker than the study's",
"effect": "The cell reads below the study's band, because a confirmation by absence is weaker than a finding. Say which marker in the note."
},
{
"key": "other",
"label": "Something else",
"effect": "Say what in the note."
}
],
"source": "Ruling OR-22 of 2026-10-29 (ledger); ledger 2026-10-22, Correction item 2"
}
