"title": "Does the academic-route condition apply to codes and standards?",
"question": "Your ruling lets an unretrievable source keep its tier and anchor only when it has a valid academic route. Does a registry citation (jurisdiction, standard number, clause) count as a valid route for a code or a standard?",
"why_yours": "Read literally, the condition removes the tier and the anchoring of every unretrievable code and standard, because codes are not cited in academic literature.",
"context": "You confirmed that an unretrievable source keeps its tier and may still anchor, earning no band above the empty circle, but only when it is properly cited in an academic context.\n\nA paper or a report has an academic route: a DOI, an index record or a citing bibliography. A code or a standard is cited by jurisdiction, number and clause, and it is not found in academic indices. As of 2026-10-29 six held sources are unverified: one T1 paper, one T4 standard and four T6 codes, and none of the five regulatory ones has a DOI. Re-derive with: select ref_id, tier, verification_method, doi from evidence_sources where verification_status = 'UNVERIFIED'.\n\nNo source states a preference between the options below.",
